<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudaki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 858–941</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abu Abdullah Rudaki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rudaki was a Persian poet at the Samanid court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, often called the father of Persian poetry. Born near Panjakent in modern Tajikistan, he enjoyed the patronage of the emir Nasr II and is said to have composed a vast body of verse, though only a small part survives. He is celebrated as a founder of classical Persian literature and as the national poet of Tajikistan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudaki.jpg" alt="Rudaki" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hossein Behzad · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniature_of_Rudaki_by_Hossein_Behzad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
